WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.320
Movies are like games of Jenga: if just one piece&nbsp;
is out of place, the entire thing collapses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.920
no matter how well built the rest of it is.&nbsp;
Even movies that otherwise were great have&nbsp;

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:13.840
been ruined by a bad decision. Here's a look at&nbsp;
movies that were destroyed by just one scene...

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.920
The Dark Knight Rises was full of&nbsp;
little head-scratching moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.360
such as "If the villain's plan is
to blow up Gotham after holding it hostage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:24.400
why does he wait all those months to do it?" Most&nbsp;
of these you can just let slide, but the reveal of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:26.680
Joseph Gordon-Levitt's real
identity?

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.600
That's...that's just too much.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.280
You know, Robin! Of course, in the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:37.080
Robin's real name is Dick. Or Jason. Or Tim. Or&nbsp;
Damien. Or even Stephanie. But apparently any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.440
of those names would have been too subtle&nbsp;
for movie audiences, so they hit you over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.480
the head instead. It's like revealing this&nbsp;
dude's name is "John Aquaman" or something.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:50.000
At the climax of Christopher Nolan's sci-fi&nbsp;
epic Interstellar, Matthew McConaughey's pilot&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.720
character falls into a black hole and lands&nbsp;
inside a far-flung future device that lets him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.240
send cryptic messages to his daughter in the past.&nbsp;
Or in other words, he has to use some hippy-dippy&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.880
Morse Code machine to save the future&nbsp;
using — that's right — the power of love.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:13.200
"No, no, no, no, Murph!! MURPH!!!! No! No!"

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.600
Really? Love? That's the secret&nbsp;
to unlocking the mysteries&nbsp;

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.280
of the time-space continuum?

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.320
"Great Scott!"

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.600
There was a minute there where it looked like&nbsp;
Ridley Scott's Prometheus was going to be one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:27.440
of the coolest movies ever made. But what&nbsp;
it turned out to be was a slow exercise in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.400
cluelessness. Plenty of fans either love it or&nbsp;
hate it, but it's hard to argue about that one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.760
sequence where the characters have to&nbsp;
avoid being crushed by a rolling spaceship.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:38.840
It looks tense, exciting, thrilling&nbsp;
even — right up until you ask yourself:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.600
why didn't they just move to the side instead of&nbsp;
trying to outrun it? In fact, the main character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.560
lives simply by rolling three feet. Now if only&nbsp;
she had told her buddy to do the same thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.560
maybe we wouldn't have left the
theater feeling profoundly stupider.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.640
Throughout the majority of 12 Years a Slave,&nbsp;
the story of Solomon Northup, a free man who&nbsp;

00:01:55.640 --> 00:02:00.040
was kidnapped and forced into slavery, is artfully&nbsp;
told through brutal visuals and compelling&nbsp;

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.200
performances. Then Brad Pitt shows up.&nbsp;
And he speaks, using whatever that accent&nbsp;

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.920
this was supposed to be, with his Brad&nbsp;
Pitt acting and his Brad Pitt face:

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:15.000
"Freedom is everything. The fact that I can even&nbsp;
walk out here tomorrow brings me great pleasure."

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.760
Uh...start walking, Brad. That&nbsp;
would give us great pleasure.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.640
Let the Right One In is a rare kind of horror&nbsp;
movie, a haunting character study with few&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:30.320
loud moments or overt scares. It is, simply&nbsp;
put, an excellent, creepy flick. But it does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:39.560
have one massive, unintentional belly&nbsp;
laugh moment that kinda ruins it...

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:45.360
*screams*

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.240
Here's a free tip to any future&nbsp;
horror movie directors. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:52.440
want to keep your audience terrified,
and on the edge of their seats, just skip the evil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:57.800
attack kittens. Kittens can't really pull off the&nbsp;
evil thing, y'know? They're just too damn cute!

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.760
"I'm going to kill you!"

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:07.440
In 1997, fans were excited at the chance to&nbsp;
see the remastered Star Wars trilogy on the&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.040
big screen. And then...this happened.

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.200
"I bet you have."

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.520
In an instant, people realized&nbsp;
George Lucas hadn't improved the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.760
series with the Special Editions.&nbsp;
He'd ruined it. The collective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:23.240
scream of distraught fans is still&nbsp;
reverberating around the universe.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:25.920
"I felt a great disturbance in the Force. As if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:30.640
millions of voices suddenly cried out&nbsp;
in terror and were suddenly silenced."

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.960
Well...maybe not exactly "silenced."

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:38.360
Now, you can almost understand the thought&nbsp;
process behind having Greedo shoot first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.800
But for every revision Lucas has explained,&nbsp;
there are at least three others made to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:48.400
trilogy are just random and bizarre. And none of&nbsp;
them are more random, or more bizarre, than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.960
insanely stupid "Jedi Rocks" dance number in the&nbsp;
middle of Return of the Jedi. Prepare yourselves:

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.199
"I have a very bad feeling about this."

00:03:56.199 --> 00:04:05.483
(horrible music playing)

00:04:05.483 --> 00:04:06.490
"No!"

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:10.920
"Nooooo!
No!"

00:04:14.229 --> 00:04:16.981
"I don't really have a lot of&nbsp;
awards, to be very honest with you."

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.040
To say that Peter Parker's dance scene ruined&nbsp;
Spider-Man 3 is a bit of an exaggeration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:28.240
since there's so much more wrong with the film.&nbsp;
The misfired Sandman CGI, the underwhelming Venom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.760
and James Franco's pie-eating performance all&nbsp;
contributed to the flop that was Spider-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:38.480
3. But all of that pales in comparison to Peter&nbsp;
Parker dancing at the jazz club. Tobey Maguire's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.480
awkward hip-thrusting and finger-snapping as&nbsp;
he gazes through his messy bangs play more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.760
like a bad SNL sketch than anything that ever&nbsp;
should have made it into a superhero movie.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:49.800
"Now dig on this."

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.560
How many people choked on their popcorn when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.480
actually said that? Because&nbsp;
seriously. That's awful.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:59.040
The scene where Neo fights a crapload of Agent&nbsp;
Smiths was supposed to be one of the major action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.440
set pieces for The Matrix Reloaded. But the second&nbsp;
the fighting begins, both Neo and Agent Smith get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:09.240
replaced with CGI so terrible it makes The Sims&nbsp;
look like virtual reality. The scene was meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:13.960
follow up the groundbreaking "bullet-time" effects&nbsp;
of the first movie. Instead, the botched effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:18.520
proved to audiences that neither Matrix sequel&nbsp;
would even come close to touching the original.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:22.280
John McClane has done a lot of wildly&nbsp;
unrealistic things in his accidental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:26.400
encounters with terrorists. But none have even&nbsp;
come close to single-handedly fighting—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:31.760
beating—an actual fighter jet. Live Free or Die&nbsp;
Hard is far from the best movie of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:36.760
but it was still pretty good even after being&nbsp;
watered down from an R-rating to PG-13. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:40.920
until the filmmakers just said, "eff it," and&nbsp;
went to crazytown in this scene. There's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:45.520
laughable ease with which a hacker takes command&nbsp;
of the Marine jet, to the Rube Goldberg-esque way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:49.960
the highway perfectly falls apart to let McClane&nbsp;
escape, the entire scene should've stayed in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:54.240
imagination of the six-year-old who thought&nbsp;
it up. Also, how is McClane able to drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.120
a semi? You know what? Never mind. That's&nbsp;
actually the least ridiculous thing here.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:01.920
With the help of a parachute, a piece of a&nbsp;
plane, and video effects that would embarrass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:07.000
a high school A/V club, James Bond surfs on what&nbsp;
appears to be a hundred foot wave of arctic water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:11.280
in Die Another Day. This single scene has been&nbsp;
cited as the death knell for Pierce Brosnan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:11.978
Bond. It also sent the entire franchise back to&nbsp;
the drawing board to reimagine the character and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.978 --> 00:06:15.600
the entire franchise. It took four years and the&nbsp;
casting of Daniel Craig for the series to redeem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:19.280
itself from that time a James Bond movie looked&nbsp;
like it was ripped from a PlayStation 2 video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:24.840
game cutscene. But we'll never forget. Your&nbsp;
Bond died that day, Pierce. Make no mistake.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:29.400
Of the many things that catch blame for&nbsp;
"ruining" the Star Wars prequels—Jar Jar Binks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.440
midi-chlorians, almost every line&nbsp;
of dialogue George Lucas wrote for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:36.520
Padme and Anakin—there is one moment&nbsp;
that makes almost every fan cringe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:41.040
no matter how dedicated. We're talking about&nbsp;
Anakin Skywalker's charred transformation into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:45.240
Darth Vader, literally the jumping-off&nbsp;
point of the entire Star Wars saga.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:48.320
In this moment, Vader learns he's lost&nbsp;
his wife and unborn children...and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:54.720
has been transformed into, like, a Space&nbsp;
Robocop. So, what does he do? He does this:

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:58.000
“Nooooooooooo”

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.440
It felt silly when it should have been&nbsp;
tear-jerking, and it capped a performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.800
by Hayden Christensen that was so unlikeable&nbsp;
that he went from that guy poised to be this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.080
generation's Harrison Ford to the guy who was in&nbsp;
that bank heist movie with Chris Brown and T.I.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:13.760
If you ever found yourself being attacked by&nbsp;
a velociraptor, you'd probably wet your pants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:17.960
and run away screaming. With that in mind, it's&nbsp;
impossible not to lose total faith in Jurassic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:21.480
Park: The Lost World when Kelly Malcolm,&nbsp;
the spunky daughter of Dr. Ian Malcolm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:24.800
stands her ground against one of the lethal&nbsp;
dinosaurs by putting her gymnastic training to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.880
the test. Watching a kid hold her own against&nbsp;
one of the franchise's nastiest villains is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:33.760
undeniably cool, but the way she does it—hauling&nbsp;
off and kicking the creature through a window—is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:38.240
entirely unbelievable. Sure, this is a movie&nbsp;
about about dinosaurs being brought back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:42.240
life. But dino-fighting-gymnastic&nbsp;
skills are just a bridge too far.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.120
The Lord of the Rings trilogy's leisurely&nbsp;
pace is part of its charm. When you watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:49.960
one of these movies, you know you're settling&nbsp;
in for a truly epic adventure. But even by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:52.520
those standards, the concluding&nbsp;
chapter, The Return of the King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:56.840
goes on for what seems like forever—especially&nbsp;
in the final act, which seems to have, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:01.000
four or five endings. It's always hard&nbsp;
to say goodbye to a great cinematic saga,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:05.960
so we can't begrudge director Peter Jackson his&nbsp;
fond Frodo farewell. Except for the scene where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:10.520
the Hobbits frolic on top of a bed in gauzy light&nbsp;
and slow motion. After spending all those hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:15.840
with Frodo, Samwise, and the rest of the gang,&nbsp;
we want to be laughing with them. Not at them.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:19.960
Most people have never gone to a Transformers&nbsp;
movie expecting a sensible plot or characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:24.640
worth caring about. We line up for these things&nbsp;
for awesome special effects, and director Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:29.800
Bay is more than happy to deliver. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
Bay isn't always content to just blow stuff up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:34.200
Sometimes he wants to be funny, too. And that's&nbsp;
what led to the introduction of Skids and Mudflap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:39.200
in Revenge of the Fallen. Forget about robots in&nbsp;
disguise: these two are racist caricatures out in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.920
the open, trading on every offensive stereotype&nbsp;
in the book. Bay later tried to throw the voice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:47.720
actors under the bus by blaming the characters'&nbsp;
demeanor on faulty line readings, but no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:52.080
bought it. Skids and Mudflap were even cut from&nbsp;
the next movie in the franchise, Dark of the Moon.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:56.200
By the time Indiana Jones and the Kingdom of&nbsp;
the Crystal Skull arrived in 2008, we'd been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:00.640
waiting for a new Indy adventure for nearly 20&nbsp;
years. Almost nothing could've killed our buzz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.640
for getting to spend another two hours with our&nbsp;
favorite archaeologist. In the end, while it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:09.420
no one's favorite Indiana Jones movie, most folks&nbsp;
were willing to forgive most of its flaws—except&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:15.160
for the infamous "nuking the fridge" sequence.&nbsp;
Even in a movie that asks us to suspend our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:20.200
disbelief far enough to accept Indy making contact&nbsp;
with aliens, this awful action set piece—which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:24.840
finds Indy miraculously surviving a nuclear&nbsp;
test blast by using a lead-lined refrigerator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:29.794
as shelter—is laughable, and ranks as one of&nbsp;
the few true missteps in a beloved franchise.

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:33.360
The first four Superman movies&nbsp;
weren't all great films by any means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:36.560
but they had a certain sweetness about them&nbsp;
that reflected the character's optimism in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:40.720
the comics. All that changed when Zack&nbsp;
Snyder took the reins for 2013's Man of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:45.280
Steel. Although darker overtones were probably&nbsp;
to be expected in the post-Dark Knight world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:49.160
it was still difficult for many longtime fans to&nbsp;
accept the rampant, casual destruction Superman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:53.680
helped unleash during his battle with Zod in the&nbsp;
final act. Even harder to accept was his decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:59.120
to end the fight...by snapping Zod's neck. Snyder&nbsp;
has defended his decision repeatedly, but that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:04.080
plot point remains the focus of intense debate.&nbsp;
For some fans, it undermines the entire concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:09.000
of the character, and when they saw it go down in&nbsp;
the theater, they had one unavoidable reaction:

